::ALG2_U02_L01_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the degree of f(x) = -4x<sup>2</sup> + x - 1? {
=2 #Correct. Highest exponent is 2.
~1 #Not the highest exponent.
~-4 #This is the leading coefficient.
~0 #The function is not constant.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the leading coefficient of f(x) = 5x<sup>2</sup> - 3x + 8? {
~2 #This is the degree.
=5 #Correct. Coefficient of x squared is 5.
~-3 #This is the x coefficient.
~8 #This is constant.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is a quadratic polynomial? {
~3x + 2 #This is linear.
~x<sup>3</sup> + 1 #This is cubic.
=7x<sup>2</sup> - x + 4 #Correct. Degree 2.
~5 #This is constant.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which form best shows the vertex of f(x) = 2(x - 1)<sup>2</sup> + 7? {
~standard form #Standard form does not directly display the vertex coordinates.
~factored form #Shows zeros.
~expanded form #Same as standard.
=vertex form #Correct. Vertex form shows (h, k).
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the y-intercept of f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> + 4x - 6? {
=-6 #Correct. f(0) = -6.
~4 #This is the x coefficient.
~1 #This is leading coefficient.
~0 #f(0) is not 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What are the zeros of f(x) = (x + 6)(x - 2)? {
~x = 6 and x = -2 #The signs are reversed.
=x = -6 and x = 2 #Correct. Set each factor equal to 0.
~x = 0 and x = 2 #x = 0 is not a zero.
~x = -6 and x = -2 #Only one value is correct.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the vertex of f(x) = (x + 4)<sup>2</sup> - 10? {
~(4, -10) #The sign is reversed.
~(-4, 10) #The k-value is wrong.
=(-4, -10) #Correct. Vertex is (-4, -10).
~(10, -4) #Coordinates are reversed.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which quadratic opens downward? {
~f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> + 2 #Positive leading coefficient opens upward.
~f(x) = 4x<sup>2</sup> - 1 #Positive leading coefficient opens upward.
~f(x) = 2(x - 1)<sup>2</sup> #Positive leading factor opens upward.
=f(x) = -3x<sup>2</sup> + 5 #Correct. Negative leading coefficient opens downward.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What does the zero x = 5 mean for f(x)? {
=f(5) = 0 #Correct. A zero is an input with output 0.
~f(5) = 5 #A zero means output 0.
~f(0) = 5 #This describes a y-intercept.
~f(0) = 0 #This is not x = 5.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which function has zeros -2 and 3? {
~f(x) = (x - 2)(x + 3) #This gives 2 and -3.
=f(x) = (x + 2)(x - 3) #Correct. The factors give zeros -2 and 3.
~f(x) = (x + 2)(x + 3) #This gives -2 and -3.
~f(x) = (x - 2)(x - 3) #This gives 2 and 3.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the axis of symmetry for zeros x = 2 and x = 8? {
~x = 2 #The axis is the midpoint.
~x = 8 #The axis is the midpoint.
=x = 5 #Correct. The average is 5.
~x = 10 #This adds without averaging.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the standard form of f(x) = (x - 2)(x + 5)? {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 3x - 10 #The linear sign is wrong.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 7x + 10 #The constant is wrong.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 10 #This omits the linear term.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 3x - 10 #Correct. Combine like terms.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which form shows the y-intercept most directly? {
=standard form #Correct. Standard form shows c.
~vertex form #Vertex form shows vertex.
~factored form #Factored form shows zeros.
~recursive form #This is not the usual quadratic form.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = 2(x - 3)(x + 1), what are the zeros? {
~x = -3 and x = 1 #The signs are reversed.
=x = 3 and x = -1 #Correct. Set each factor equal to 0.
~x = 2 and x = 3 #The leading factor is not a zero.
~x = 0 and x = 2 #These values do not make factors zero.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the constant term in f(x) = 6x<sup>2</sup> - x + 12? {
~6 #This is leading coefficient.
~-1 #This is the x coefficient.
=12 #Correct. The constant has no variable.
~2 #This is the degree.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is not a polynomial function? {
~x<sup>2</sup> + 1 #This is a polynomial.
~4x - 9 #This is a polynomial.
~-5 #This is a constant polynomial.
=3/x + 2 #Correct. x in the denominator is not polynomial form.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What are the x-intercepts of f(x) = (x - 1)(x - 4)? {
=(1, 0) and (4, 0) #Correct. The zeros are 1 and 4.
~(-1, 0) and (-4, 0) #The signs are reversed.
~(0, 1) and (0, 4) #These are not x-intercepts.
~(1, 4) and (0, 0) #This mixes coordinates.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the leading coefficient of f(x) = -x<sup>2</sup> + 7x - 4? {
~1 #The sign is negative.
=-1 #Correct. The coefficient of x squared is -1.
~7 #This is x coefficient.
~-4 #This is constant term.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is true about f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> + 1? {
~It is not a polynomial. #It is polynomial.
~It has degree 1. #Highest exponent is 2.
=It is a quadratic polynomial. #Correct. It is degree 2.
~It has leading coefficient 0. #Leading coefficient is 1.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the vertex of f(x) = -2(x - 6)<sup>2</sup> + 1? {
~(-6, 1) #The sign is reversed.
~(1, 6) #Coordinates are reversed.
~(6, -1) #The k-value is positive.
=(6, 1) #Correct. Vertex is (6, 1).
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which feature is easiest to identify from f(x) = a(x - r)(x - s)? {
=zeros #Correct. The factors reveal zeros.
~vertex #Vertex is not direct.
~axis only #Axis requires more work.
~range only #Range requires vertex.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is f(0) for f(x) = 3x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 11? {
~3 #This is leading coefficient.
=11 #Correct. f(0) is 11.
~-2 #This is x coefficient.
~0 #Constant is not 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>A quadratic is a polynomial function with what degree? {
~1 #Degree 1 is linear.
~3 #Degree 3 is cubic.
=2 #Correct. Quadratics have degree 2.
~0 #Degree 0 is constant.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which representation helps connect a quadratic to polynomial behavior? {
~Only a table #A table alone is limited.
~Only a verbal description #A description alone is limited.
~Only a calculator screen #A calculator screen alone is limited.
=Equivalent algebraic forms and graph features #Correct. Forms and features show structure.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L01_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x) = -x<sup>2</sup> + 4, which statement is true? {
=The graph opens downward. #Correct. Negative opens downward.
~The graph opens upward. #The leading coefficient is negative.
~The degree is 1. #The degree is 2.
~The leading coefficient is 4. #The leading coefficient is -1.
}



